  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "Why Does My Wall Paint Look Patchy After Touch-Ups?",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "HH Remodeling LLC"
  },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "HH Remodeling LLC",
    "url": "http://coloradoremodeling.company"
  },
  "articleBody": "It's super frustrating when you try to fix a small paint blemish, only to have the touched-up spot stand out even more, right? This often happens for a few key reasons, and it's something we see a lot. One common culprit is using paint from a different batch than the original, or even an old can that's been sitting around for ages. Paint formulations can shift slightly, and over time, the color might change. Another big one is using the wrong tool; if the original paint was rolled, but you're dabbing with a brush, the texture will be off, making it super noticeable. Applying too much paint is also a frequent mistake. You want a very thin, feathered application to blend, not a thick blob. And finally, not matching the gloss or sheen of the original paint is a dead giveaway. Even if the color's perfect, a different sheen will make the repair pop. To get a truly invisible fix, sometimes you just need an expert eye. If your touch-up just isn't blending, give HH Remodeling LLC a call. We can help you achieve that seamless finish you're looking for.",
